 ## Overview
-This article details four architectural modifications to the HashNeRF color and density networks, evaluating residual connections, convolutional layers, and network depth on reconstruction quality and training time [S1, unofficial]. These variants modify the standard shallow multi-layer perceptron (MLP) decoder by altering internal connectivity and layer types [S1, unofficial].
+HashNeRF Architectural Variants are specific modifications to the shallow neural network decoder used in HashNeRF, designed to optimize the trade-off between rendering accuracy (PSNR) and training time [S1, unofficial]. While the standard HashNeRF uses a simple multi-layer perceptron (MLP) with linear layers, these variants explore convolutional layers, residual connections, and deeper network structures to improve feature extraction and color prediction [S1, unofficial]. They differ from sibling methods like Zip-NeRF by focusing on architectural ablation of the MLP decoder rather than changing the underlying hash encoding or density estimation mechanism [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## How it works
-The base HashNeRF method [8678-hashnerf.md] maps spatial coordinates to a high-dimensional feature vector using multi-resolution hash encoding, which is then processed by a shallow MLP [S1, unofficial]. The variants below modify this MLP structure [S1, unofficial]. All variants use the Mean Squared Error (MSE) loss over rendered rays [S1, unofficial]. The variants are evaluated under matched iteration budgets (1,000 iterations) on synthetic scenes (e.g., Lego) with reduced view counts (100 training views) [S1, unofficial].
+The variants modify the "Shallow Neural Network" stage of HashNeRF, which takes the hashed feature vector as input and outputs density ($\sigma$) and color ($C$) [S1, unofficial]. The input to these networks is the concatenated feature vector from the multi-resolution hash encoding. The outputs are typically a scalar for density and a 3-channel vector for color (RGB) [S1, unofficial].
+
+The following four variants were proposed and evaluated on the synthetic Lego scene (100 training views, 200 test views) with a downsampling factor of 4, trained for 1,000 iterations [S1, unofficial].
 
