 ## Subtopics
 
-### HashNeRF Variant 2
-A specific configuration of the residual connection strategy where the network explicitly concatenates the original input views to intermediate layer outputs in a two-pipeline structure for sigma and color networks [S1, unofficial]. It achieves a PSNR of 25.873 with a training time of 306.751 [S1, unofficial].
+### [Switch-NeRF++ Forward Pass and Loss](17395-switch-nerf-forward-pass-and-loss.md)
+Switch-NeRF++ is a large-scale neural radiance field method that employs a Mixture of Experts (MoE) architecture to efficiently render heterogeneous urban scenes. It solves the scalability problem of HashGrid Neural Fields by routing 3D points to specialized hash experts based on local scene complexity, thereby balancing memory usage and rendering accuracy. It differs from standard HashNeRF by introducing a learned gating mechanism that selects a single expert per point, and from Switch-NeRF by upgrading the expert capacity and resolution scaling for higher fidelity in massive environments [17395-switch-nerf-forward-pass-and-loss].
 
-### HashNeRF Variant 1
-A configuration replacing linear layers with 1D convolutions (kernel size 3) to reduce parameters [S1, unofficial]. It consists of a single pipeline with four convolutional layers [S1, unofficial]. It yields the lowest PSNR (15.831) among the variants but has a longer training time (363.415) [S1, unofficial].
+### [Switch-NeRF++ Gating](17411-switch-nerf-gating.md)
+Switch-NeRF++ Gating is a mechanism for selecting a single expert from a collection of heterogeneous hash experts based on the input 3D point. It solves the problem of efficiently routing spatial data to specialized representations, enabling joint optimization of experts and the gating network through direct gradient-based training. It is distinguished from its sibling [Switch-NeRF++ Forward Pass and Loss](17395-switch-nerf-forward-pass-and-loss.md) by its specific use of hash encoding within the gating network itself, rather than a pure MLP, which improves efficiency and consistency with the hash experts.
 
-### HashNeRF Variant 3
-A configuration using residual connections from alternate layers (2 layers prior) in a two-pipeline structure [S1, unofficial]. It aims to balance information preservation and parameter tuning [S1, unofficial]. It achieves a PSNR of 26.547 with a training time of 305.208 [S1, unofficial].
-
-### HashNeRF Variant 4
-A deeper configuration adding 3 linear layers and residual connections from sigma layers [S1, unofficial]. It achieves the highest PSNR (27.119) among the variants with a training time of 319.669 [S1, unofficial].
+### [GP-NeRF](17415-gp-nerf.md)
+GP-NeRF is a unified learning framework that embeds a Neural Radiance Field (NeRF) and a 2D segmentation module to perform context-aware 3D scene perception. It jointly constructs radiance and semantic-embedding fields, facilitating joint volumetric rendering to ensure geometry distribution consistency between the two fields. Unlike prior methods that render semantic labels per pixel independently, GP-NeRF renders rich-semantic features and feeds them into a 2D segmentation module for context-aware perception.
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Training times range from 305.208 to 363.415 for 1,000 iterations [S1, unofficial]. Variant 1 is the slowest; Variants 2 and 3 are the fastest.
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Not given in the sources.
-*   **Measured Performance:** PSNR ranges from 15.831 (Variant 1) to 27.119 (Variant 4) on the Lego scene with 100 training views [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Variants 2-4 offer improved PSNR over the baseline or Variant 1 by better preserving input information or increasing capacity [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** None of the extensions conclusively outperform published baselines under iso-time evaluation [S1, unofficial]. Variant 1 performs poorly [S1, unofficial].
-*   **When to use:** Use Variant 4 for highest quality under constrained iterations. Use Variant 2 or 3 for faster convergence. Avoid Variant 1 unless parameter reduction is critical and quality is secondary [S1, unofficial].
+The variants were evaluated on the synthetic Lego scene with 100 training views and 200 test views, with data downsampled by a factor of 4 [S1, unofficial]. Training was conducted for 1,000 iterations [S1, unofficial].
+
+*   **Variant 1 (Conv1d):** PSNR 15.831, Training Time 363.415s. This variant performed poorly in terms of accuracy compared to linear variants, despite the goal of reducing parameters [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Variant 2 (Residual from Input):** PSNR 25.873, Training Time 306.751s. This variant showed significant improvement over Variant 1 and the baseline, with faster training than Variant 1 [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Variant 3 (Residual from Alternate Layers):** PSNR 26.547, Training Time 305.208s. This variant improved accuracy over Variant 2 with similar training time, validating the benefit of alternate-layer residuals [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Variant 4 (Deeper Network):** PSNR 27.119, Training Time 319.669s. This variant achieved the highest PSNR among the variants, indicating that increased depth and parameters can improve color output, at the cost of slightly longer training time [S1, unofficial].
+
+Under iso-time evaluation, none of the extensions conclusively outperformed published baselines, but they characterize which extensions transfer to constrained settings [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-These variants were proposed in a study evaluating low-cost Neural Radiance Fields, specifically ablation studies on the feature-decoding MLP of HashNeRF [S1, unofficial]. The work was conducted to understand the effect of input downsampling and architectural changes on PSNR and runtime under reduced view counts (100 training views) [S1, unofficial]. The experiments were performed on the synthetic Lego scene from the NeRF dataset [S1, unofficial].
+These architectural variants were proposed in the context of evaluating low-cost neural radiance fields, specifically abalating the feature-decoding MLP of TensoRF and studying HashNeRF designs [S1, unofficial]. The work aims to understand how architectural changes in the shallow decoder affect performance in resource-constrained environments (reduced view counts, downsampled data) [S1, unofficial]. The multi-level hash encoding model has applications in gigapixel approximation, signed distance function generation, and neural radiance caching, but was customized here for NeRF generation [S1, unofficial].
 
